# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, "A Quick Example" (the program abs), and exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 a).
id: absolute-value
title: Absolute value
topic: Conditional
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, "A Quick Example"; exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 a
tags: conditional, wp

task:
  Prove that the program computes the absolute value of $x$ in $y$.

pre: true
post: y >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> y = x) && (x < 0 ==> y = -x)

program:
  {P}
  if x < 0 then {
    {P && x < 0}
    {R1}
    y := -x           // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {P && !(x < 0)}
    {R2}
    y := x            // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The then branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(y := -x, Q)
  = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x) && (x < 0 ==> -x = -x)
set: R1 = -x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> -x = x) && (x < 0 ==> -x = -x)
vc: true && x < 0 ==> R1
why:
  Assume $x < 0$. Then $-x > 0$, so $-x >= 0$. The implication
  $x >= 0 ==> -x = x$ holds because its left side is false. The last
  conjunct holds because $-x = -x$ is always true.

step: The else branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(y := x, Q)
  = x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> x = x) && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)
set: R2 = x >= 0 && (x >= 0 ==> x = x) && (x < 0 ==> x = -x)
vc: true && !(x < 0) ==> R2
why:
  Assume $!(x < 0)$, that is $x >= 0$. The first conjunct is the
  assumption. The second holds since $x = x$. The third holds since its
  left side $x < 0$ is false.

step: Conclude with the rule for the conditional
rule: IF
instance: IF
  {true && x < 0} y := -x {Q}
  {true && !(x < 0)} y := x {Q}
  ---
  {true} if x < 0 then y := -x else y := x {Q}
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  In each branch, one of the two implications of the postcondition is
  true because its left side contradicts the condition of the branch. This
  is how the knowledge $B$ or $!B$ of the rule IF is used.
